1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,150
Time to own up.
是时候坦白了。

2
00:00:02,150 --> 00:00:06,410
When you‘re sitting at your computer, do you work solidly all day?
当你坐在电脑前时，你是一整天都在踏踏实实地工作吗？

3
00:00:06,410 --> 00:00:20,700
Or do you allow yourself to become distracted now and again... to open a few extra tabs, check social media, read a spot of news, browse the latest fashion trends, maybe catch up on some celebrity gossip, and look at some...
还是会时不时地让自己分心......打开几个额外的标签页，查看社交媒体，读一点新闻，浏览最新的时尚潮流，也许还会了解一些名人八卦，或者看看其他的......

4
00:00:20,700 --> 00:00:21,590
Stop!
停！

5
00:00:21,590 --> 00:00:23,490
You’re ’cyberloafing‘.
你这是在“网络摸鱼”。

6
00:00:23,490 --> 00:00:31,960
The unwieldy word is a combination of ’cyber‘, which means ’related to computers‘, and ’loafing‘, which means ’relaxing in a lazy way‘.
这个拗口的词是由“cyber”（意思是“与计算机有关的”）和“loafing”（意思是“以懒散的方式放松”）组合而成的。

7
00:00:31,960 --> 00:00:34,540
We do it, and we do it a lot.
我们会这么做，而且还经常这么做。

8
00:00:34,540 --> 00:00:41,770
A study from the University of Texas suggests we are guilty of this form of procrastination for 14% of our working day.
德克萨斯大学的一项研究表明，在我们的工作日中，有14%的时间都在犯这种拖延的毛病。

9
00:00:41,770 --> 00:00:44,850
On a Friday afternoon, I expect it’s more than that.
我猜在周五下午，这个比例会更高。

10
00:00:44,850 --> 00:00:48,920
I‘ll admit to indulging in the odd bout of cyberloafing myself.
我承认自己也会偶尔沉迷于网络摸鱼。

11
00:00:48,920 --> 00:00:52,160
In fact I’ve already done it in the course of writing this story.
事实上，在写这篇文章的过程中我就已经这么做了。

12
00:00:52,160 --> 00:00:54,160
OK, more than once.
好吧，不止一次。

13
00:00:54,160 --> 00:00:56,280
But it‘s a slippery slope.
但这是一个滑坡效应。

14
00:00:56,280 --> 00:00:59,640
It can damage your productivity and even your career.
它会损害你的工作效率，甚至影响你的职业生涯。

15
00:00:59,640 --> 00:01:09,030
Because according to the Univeristy of Texas research, each time a worker gets sidetracked by the internet it takes an average of 23 minutes to get back to work.
因为根据德克萨斯大学的研究，每次员工被网络转移注意力后，平均需要23分钟才能重新回到工作状态。

16
00:01:09,030 --> 00:01:17,400
More than that, cyberloafing has a dark side, according to a report published in The Journal of Psychosocial Research on Cyberspace.
不仅如此，根据发表在《网络空间心理社会研究杂志》上的一份报告，网络摸鱼还有其阴暗面。

17
00:01:17,400 --> 00:01:22,690
It argues that cyberloafing is connected to several negative personality traits.
报告指出，网络摸鱼与一些负面的人格特质有关。

18
00:01:22,690 --> 00:01:28,480
People who cyberloaf are more likely to be manipulative, self-interested and narcissistic.
网络摸鱼的人更有可能具有操控欲、自私自利和自恋的特点。

19
00:01:28,480 --> 00:01:36,020
Furthermore, people with these traits are less likely to feel remorse about avoiding work, and less concerned about getting caught.
此外，具有这些特质的人不太可能对逃避工作感到懊悔，也不太担心被发现。

20
00:01:36,020 --> 00:01:37,820
Why do we do it?
我们为什么会这么做呢？

21
00:01:37,820 --> 00:01:42,970
For some, it’s undoubtedly an escape from the drudgery of what they‘re supposed to be doing.
对一些人来说，这无疑是一种逃避他们本该做的枯燥乏味工作的方式。

22
00:01:42,970 --> 00:01:46,660
Or a way of avoiding tackling that ever growing in-tray.
或者是一种避免处理那不断增多的待办事项的方法。

23
00:01:46,660 --> 00:01:52,040
Some might get a feeling of satisfaction, get a buzz from finding a great deal online.
有些人可能会从中获得满足感，从在网上找到大优惠中得到兴奋感。

24
00:01:52,040 --> 00:01:59,170
But overall, it’s something that takes you away from what you‘re actually being paid to do, or paying to do: if you’re a student.
但总的来说，这会让你偏离你实际上被付钱去做，或者你花钱去做的事情，如果你是学生的话。

25
00:01:59,170 --> 00:02:03,890
When you have the whole world of the internet at your fingertips, it‘s hard to resist.
当整个互联网世界触手可及时，这很难抗拒。

26
00:02:03,890 --> 00:02:05,890
How do we nip it in the bud?
我们要如何防患于未然呢？

27
00:02:05,890 --> 00:02:11,990
If you can truly ask yourself why you are cyberloafing, then that can help identify the base problem.
如果你能真正问问自己为什么会网络摸鱼，那么这有助于找出根本问题。

28
00:02:11,990 --> 00:02:17,210
And if that sounds like too much hard work, you could just switch off the internet for a couple of hours!
如果这听起来太费劲，你可以直接关掉网络几个小时！

29
00:02:17,210 --> 00:02:21,240
So in the meantime, let me just close those extra tabs...
所以，在此期间，让我先关掉那些额外的标签页......
